<Base title="GATE 2027 preparation guide" description="How to prepare for GATE 2027 without coaching: syllabus-first strategy, weightage thinking, practice loops and revision." canonicalPath="/guides/preparation/">
  <section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="kicker">Strategy</p><h1>Preparation guide</h1><p class="lead">Syllabus first, fundamentals second, practice always, revision on schedule. No fake rank predictors, no urgency tricks.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap grid cols-2">
    <div class="card"><h3>The loop</h3><ol><li><strong>Learn:</strong> one topic, minimum prerequisites (<a href="/learn/">explorer</a>).</li><li><strong>Practice:</strong> original questions + PYQs by topic.</li><li><strong>Review:</strong> classify every miss (<a href="/tools/mistake-tracker/">tracker</a>).</li><li><strong>Revise:</strong> spaced schedule (<a href="/tools/revision-planner/">planner</a>).</li><li><strong>Mock:</strong> timed sets in the last 6–8 weeks (<a href="/tools/mock-analyzer/">analyser</a>).</li></ol></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Time rules</h3><ul><li>Weekly: 1 revision block + 1 practice block per subject touched.</li><li>Monthly: 1 full revision sweep + 1 timed mock (later phase).</li><li>Daily: one primary task + one practice block + one revision block.</li><li>Buffer days are part of the plan, not failure.</li></ul><p><a class="btn ghost" href="/guides/study-plan/">See a sample study plan →</a></p></div>
  </div></section>
</Base>
